{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"I accept the probe contract. It resolves my objection by making the consumer’s cursor and compound step authoritative: the probe commits nothing, and its returned value and cursor are explicitly discarded. There is no pending observation whose delivery the consumer must honor.\n\nAn eviction between probe and execution can change the answer, but the consumer re-reads and applies its existing gap policy. The probe therefore promises only “run the step again,” not successful delivery. The extra read is an acknowledged cost.\n\nTwo details should be precise in the plan:\n\n- “Re-parked after each step” means after a **non-terminal** step that needs further observation. A completed or failed consumer must retire its probe. Pending response writes must remain eligible for host cadence; source readiness must never become a prerequisite for retrying them.\n- Cursor isolation concerns the underlying cursor state, not merely reference spelling. A probe must not alias cursor state advanced by another waitset entry.\n\n“Non-advancing readiness probe” is more accurate than “pure readiness probe”: it still observes a changing medium. This terminology does not affect acceptance.\n\nWith writer migration removed and host cadence retained wherever probing does not fit, this is a sufficiently explicit observation contract for item 9.\n\nDISPUTE-B: accepted — Non-advancing advisory probes preserve compound-step ownership provided terminal probes retire, pending writes retain cadence, and probe cursors cannot alias advancing waiters."}}
